atlas capability

architecture command-law
  canonical_name CommandLawGraph
  family capability
  domain Capability / Computational Law
  layer capability
  semantic_question What computational law does this command preserve, beneath its domain?
  purpose The second axis of the estate, from the run6/run7 deep read: the 91 commands are faces of a few deep calculi -- composition, causal execution, coupled dynamics, evidence/evolution -- plus the multi-identity laws (hash/canon/graph/family/entity). Domain says what estate; law says what algebra a hop preserves, which is what makes composition sound.
  maturity implemented
  node_types Command
  node_types Law
  edge_types preserves_law
  internal_calculus law classification (composition / causal / coupled / evidence-evolution / representation / retrieval / media / governance)
  invariant a command's law is what a composed hop must preserve; the estate is what it belongs to
  forbidden_inference same domain -> same law
  forbidden_inference a law label -> a proof the algebra holds
  command BonfyreDiscipl
  source_path components/BonfyreControlPlane/command_laws.py.archive
  source_path components/BonfyreControlPlane/command_advertisements.py.archive
  witness components/BonfyreControlPlane/tests/test_command_advertisements.py.archive
  publishes CommandAdvertisement
  known_gap 27 artifact/media commands now seed native DisCIPL families and data-flow contracts; the remaining advertised estate still needs verified consumes/produces grammar before it may join composition
  interacts capability-lattice
  interacts discipl-composition
  interacts command-law-seeds-discipl
  source_lineage run6/run7 second deep read -- 91 commands as deep calculi
  known_gap every source this entry names is archived, so nothing here is a live implementation a reader can open and run. Whether a native owner has taken this work over is not established in this entry; until one is named and witnessed here, the architecture stands on retired code.
architecture witness-enforcement
  canonical_name WitnessEnforcement
  family institutional
  domain Institutional
  maturity measured
  semantic_question Is a claimed measurement backed by evidence anyone can open today?
  # The atlas states no maturity laundering as its first non-negotiable rule and
  # checked only that a witness list was non-empty. Any sentence satisfied it, so
  # `witness a bounded proof artifact` passed the guard against claiming a
  # measurement without one. The rule forbade an absent witness, not a fictional
  # one, and eight claims rested on evidence that had been archived or deleted.
  node_types Witness
  node_types MaturityClaim
  edge_types supports
  state_types live
  state_types archived
  state_types missing
  invariant a measured or proven claim carries at least one witness that can be opened or run today
  invariant a witness naming a file must have that file, with home-relative paths expanded; ten substrate proofs live outside the tree and a checker that failed to expand them would report every one as missing, which is how a false alarm becomes a reason to distrust the check
  invariant an archived witness beside a live one is historical context; an archive standing alone is a claim whose evidence was retired and never revisited
  invariant a prose witness is left alone rather than failed, because some evidence genuinely is a description of an external result -- and it is reported as unverifiable rather than counted as verification
  invariant the failure message names which kind of nothing it found; a missing file and a retired one have different fixes and naming the wrong one sends the reader to the wrong place
  forbidden_inference a listed witness -> a real one
  forbidden_inference a proof file that exists -> a proof that reproduces
  forbidden_inference state passed inside a proof -> a currently passing check
  computational_form validation
  native_format yaff
  source_path cmd/BonfyreAtlas/src/main.c
  witness bun test architecture/tests/atlas_core.test.mjs
  measured enforcement withdrew 8 of 57 measured claims: one repointed at a live suite, seven demoted to implemented because every witness they carried had been archived
  known_gap the ten substrate semantic-proofs carry an output_digest that reproduces from nothing available; re-running egglog's own program gives the asserted term and a different digest, so the proofs are readable and not re-verifiable
  known_gap 63 interactions carry prose witnesses that no check can verify; they are reported rather than failed, and nothing yet distinguishes an honest description from a placeholder
  internal_calculus a source_path is held to the same standard as a witness: a claim resting on a file that does not open rests on nothing, and the reader cannot even go and look
  internal_calculus an architecture whose every source is archived is not an implementation; it may still describe something native, and it has to say which in a known_gap rather than let the maturity imply it
  internal_calculus a witness is often a real file followed by what it proves, so the path is read from the first token and prose alone still counts -- reading it more narrowly reported two live measurements as unwitnessed
  source_path architecture/check_maturity_backing.py.archive
  source_path cmd/BonfyreAtlas/src/main.c
  witness cmd/BonfyreAtlas/tests/backing.sh verifies native validation rejects missing source paths, unexplained archive-only backing and missing or archived measurement witnesses, then accepts restored backing and explicit retirement gaps
  witness 2026-09-19 the rule found what it was written for: eight source paths naming files that do not exist in this checkout, one measurement resting entirely on another company's repository, and thirty architectures standing on archived code without saying so -- all thirty of which had passed validation for as long as the rule went unenforced
  witness 2026-09-19 removing the honest known_gap from command-law made validation fail with that architecture named, and restoring it passed; the rule is enforced rather than described
  forbidden_inference an architecture that validates -> an architecture that runs
  known_gap the thirty retired entries now say they stand on archived code; none of them yet names the native owner that took the work over, so the gap is visible rather than closed
  internal_calculus a test is not a caller: an organ can pass every assertion it makes about itself and still be reached by no path in the estate, and `implemented` says nothing about which
  source_path architecture/check-organ-callers.mjs
  source_path cmd/BonfyreAtlas/src/build_edges.h
  source_path cmd/BonfyreAtlas/build-edges.cue
  witness cmd/BonfyreAtlas/tests/build-edges.sh retains the former JS audit's text and JSON on six directory twins, covering command and component binary matches, older and alternate builds, missing installs and Atlas entries, delegation, strict mismatch refusal and recovery
  invariant build-edges reads current YAFF and reports simple Makefile source assignments, installed hashes and source modification times without changing either build or installation
  forbidden_inference matching installed bytes or a directory twin -> an obsolete capability or permission to retire either tree
  known_gap build-edges reads simple assignments rather than evaluating Makefile variables or continuations; its report preserves the previous audit's scope
  source_path architecture/check_organ_callers.py.archive
  witness architecture/tests/check-organ-callers.test.mjs proves an organ's source and tests do not count as callers and a separate command caller does
  witness 2026-09-19 check_organ_callers.py found eight of twenty-nine native organs called by nothing outside their own source, header and tests: commerce, actor, agent_contract, corroboration, occurrence_origin, retention, semantic_operator and standing_address. Callers were written for commerce and retention the same day, leaving six
  witness 2026-09-21 check_organ_callers.py indexes supported source files once, completes in under two seconds and finds every one of 29 native organs has an external caller; --strict passes
  witness cmd/BonfyreActors/tests/organ_callers_test.sh exercises the six previously-unreached native caller CLIs and their refusal boundaries
  known_gap the caller audit proves a reachable caller, not scheduled or deployed use; these six are explicit operator CLIs until their owning ingest, worker or surface path binds them.
